### 2. WireWatch: Measuring the Security of Proprietary Network Encryption in Android
*Presenter:* Mona Wang  
*Slide Deck:* [WireWatch](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-irtfopen-mona-wang-wirewatch-00)

- **Background & Motivation:** While web HTTPS adoption exceeds 80–90%, mobile app traffic often relies on proprietary protocols. Major apps (e.g., WeChat with >1.5 billion active users) use custom transport protocols like MMTLS (a custom variant based on TLS 1.3 semantics over HTTP POST). Top Chinese Input Method Editors (IMEs/keyboards) transmit keystrokes using custom, insecure encryption schemes.
- **Vulnerability Findings:**
  - Custom cryptographic protocols in keyboards exhibited textbook flaws (e.g., base64 RSA-bootstrapped AES vulnerable to CBC padding oracle attacks, timestamp-derived keys, static hardcoded keys, unpadded RSA, and unencrypted HTTP/UDP transmissions).
  - Measurement across stores: Scraped and dynamic-analysis pipeline showed ~80%+ TLS usage on Google Play, whereas the Xiaomi Store had ~66% plaintext traffic and ~50% proprietary encryption in top apps. Furthermore, 50% of TLS implementations on the Xiaomi Store failed to validate certificates properly.
  - Reverse engineering of 9 top proprietary protocol families revealed severe cryptographic vulnerabilities in 8 of them, exposing full URL browsing history and telemetry.
- **Discussion & Q&A:**
  - *Root Causes:* Legacy code from the early 2010s, developer emphasis on anti-scraping/obfuscation over standards-based transport security, and intense working schedules ("996" culture).
  - *Digital Sovereignty & CAs:* Questions were raised regarding sovereign CAs and SM cipher suite usage. Mona Wang clarified that SM cipher suites were rarely observed (<2 apps), suggesting sovereignty was not the primary driver.
  - *MMTLS Analysis:* Usama (Muhammad Usama Sardar) and Martin Thomson discussed MMTLS details in chat and Q&A. MMTLS pins keys and uses HTTP POST per request/response, requiring session resumption on every transaction, but did not exhibit the blatant cryptographic flaws found in other custom protocols.
  - *Application-Layer Security:* Martin Thomson emphasized that fixing transport security alone is insufficient if higher-layer application privacy practices remain flawed.

