### 3. Reliable and Decentralized Certificate Verification via DNS (RevDNS)
*Presenter:* Protick Bhowmick  
*Slide Deck:* [The Case for RevDNS](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-irtfopen-protick-bhowmick-the-case-for-revdns-00)

- **OCSP Limitations & Centralization:** Online Certificate Status Protocol (OCSP) introduces TLS handshake latency, privacy risks (leaking browsing habits to CAs), and soft-fail security bypasses on timeouts. Measurement data showed 12% timeout rates and extreme infrastructure centralization (62% of wild certificates relying on Akamai for OCSP response serving).
- **Core Insight & RevDNS Mechanism:**
  - Revocation is extremely sparse (e.g., Let's Encrypt had ~655k revoked out of 612M active certificates; Microsoft had ~1k revoked out of 78M).
  - CAs publish dedicated DNSSEC-signed zones (`ocsp.example.com`) containing TXT records *only* for revoked certificate serial numbers.
  - For non-revoked certificates, queries return `NXDOMAIN` alongside DNSSEC NSEC records. Leveraging aggressive negative caching (RFC 8198), recursive resolvers cache signed range proofs of non-existence.
- **Performance & Feasibility:**
  - Single NSEC records cover millions of valid contiguous serial numbers (e.g., one NSEC record covered 2.1M valid certificates for Microsoft).
  - Offloads >95% of queries to recursive resolvers, reducing cache memory footprints by ~20x while achieving TLS handshake latency savings up to 270 ms for non-CDN responders.
- **Discussion & Q&A:**
  - *Infrastructure Centralization:* Joel Jaeggli noted that CDNs hosting authoritative DNS might still maintain concentration; Protick clarified that recursive resolver caching absorbs the vast majority of requests.
  - *Alternative Revocation Mechanisms:* In response to Usama's questions regarding CRLs and OCSP stapling, Protick noted low deployment rates for OCSP stapling due to web server configuration overhead.
  - *In-Chat Discussion:* Martin Thomson and Dave Plonka discussed alternative mechanisms like Firefox's CRLite and Chromium's CRLSets, noting that RevDNS provides a clean evaluation of DNSSEC capabilities for sparse revocation sets.
  - *DANE TLSA:* Ingmar raised DANE TLSA comparisons; Protick noted RevDNS specifically targets low-overhead revocation range proofs over standard DNSSEC without changing server certificates.

