### 4. Big Dumb Networks
*Presenter:* Mark Handley  
*Slide Deck:* [Big Dumb Networks](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-irtfopen-mark-handley-big-dumb-networks-00)

- **Context & Motivation:** OpenAI supercomputer back-end networks support synchronous AI pre-training across >100,000 GPUs connected by >10 million optical links. Because computations are tightly synchronized, tail latency at the 100th percentile ($p_{100}$) dictates overall job performance.
- **Design Philosophy ("Big Dumb Network"):**
  - Remove control-plane intelligence, dynamic routing protocols (no BGP/SDN for data routing), and switch-based adaptive load balancing.
  - Use static routing tables configured at commissioning time on multi-plane Clos switches (51.2 Tbps, 512x100G radix).
- **Host-Driven Routing & Transport Protocol (MRC):**
  - **SRv6 Source Routing:** End-host NICs source-route every packet over 256 active paths using IPv6 Segment Routing (SRv6) micro-SIDs. Packets are sprayed uniformly across all available paths.
  - **Multi-path Reliable Connection (MRC):** An open standard (OCP) transport protocol co-developed with industry partners. Features direct out-of-order payload delivery to memory (DMA-style), packet trimming (separating congestive loss from link failures), and selective retransmission.
  - **Fast End-Host Failover:** When a packet loss occurs, the host transport protocol immediately demotes the failed path to a "sin bin" and replaces it with a backup path from a pre-calculated list. Link failures are mapped out within 30–40 $\mu\text{s}$ without waiting for network control plane convergence. Spreading loss across 256 paths minimizes tail-latency impact per flow.
  - **Congestion Control:** Receiver-driven, credit-based rate pacing in host software eliminates core in-network queuing/congestion, restricting bottlenecks to last-hop incasts or oversubscribed inter-building links.
- **Discussion & Q&A:**
  - *Degraded / Flapping Links:* Warren Kumari inquired about silent packet degradation. Mark explained hosts issue SRv6 loopback probes every 1 ms across all links to detect and quarantine lossy paths.
  - *Packet Reordering:* Francois Michel asked about packet reordering overhead. Mark clarified MRC writes arriving payloads directly into memory out-of-order using target memory offsets, completing transactions sequentially once all fragments land.
  - *WAN Adaptation & Internet Applications:* Lars Eggert and Mark Handley discussed applying packet spraying, trimming, and SRv6 host routing to wide-area protocols where tail latency is critical.
  - *NIC Offload Feasibility:* In response to China Mobile inquiries on NIC resource requirements, Mark noted production MRC implementations exist across NVIDIA ConnectX-8/9, AMD Pensando/Pollara, and Thor Ultra NICs.

